{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n`Makefile` line 128 (the `worktree` target) provisions the new worktree's venv with:\n\n    python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate && $(MAKE) install\n\nIt inherits whatever `python3` resolves to on the host. Nothing pins or checks the version, and\n`requires-python = \">=3.11\"` (pyproject.toml:16) accepts anything from 3.11 upward, so\n`uv sync --locked` succeeds and the mismatch is silent.\n\n## Measured on this host 2026-08-05\n\n    system python3            -> Python 3.14.6   (/opt/homebrew/bin/python3)\n    CI matrix                 -> 3.11, 3.12, 3.13   (.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml)\n    worktree c6c9-attest-split/.venv      -> Python 3.14.6\n    worktree rebar-8a5e/.venv             -> Python 3.14.6\n    worktree fix-c5b3-scc-byfile/.venv    -> Python 3.14.6\n    main checkout .venv                   -> Python 3.12.13\n\nEvery worktree created by `make worktree` runs **3.14.6, which CI never tests**. The main\ncheckout is on a tested version only by accident of WHEN it was created — its venv predates the\nhost's python3 moving to 3.14.\n\n## The cost, which is not hypothetical\n\nThis is the canonical local-vs-CI divergence, and it burned real time tonight. Multiple agents\nworking in fresh worktrees hit local test failures that CI did not reproduce, then each spent\ncycles proving them \"pre-existing and environment-specific\" -- correctly, but only after the\nfact. Two failures repeatedly implicated:\n\n    tests/unit/grounding/test_coverage_reconstruction.py::test_full_backend_account_is_present\n    tests/unit/metrics/test_jscpd_dup_heldout.py::test_live_clone\n\nEach agent rediscovered this independently. The failure mode is corrosive rather than loud:\nlocal red that CI does not show trains agents (and humans) to discount local failures\ngenerally, which is exactly backwards -- the next genuine local-only regression gets waved\nthrough with the same reasoning.\n\nIt also cuts the other way: a change that breaks on 3.11 but passes on the developer's 3.14\nlooks clean locally and only fails after push, which is the slowest possible place to learn it.\n\n## Note on scope\n\nThe version floor is separately declared and enforced for **git** (ticket 980d added\n`.github/git-version-floor.txt` plus a conftest session-abort). There is no equivalent for\nPython, even though Python is the far more likely source of behavioral divergence.\n\n## Candidate approaches (weigh; not prescribed)\n\n1. Pin the worktree venv to a CI-tested interpreter, e.g. `uv venv --python 3.12` (uv can fetch\n   it), so a fresh worktree matches the matrix by construction.\n2. Warn or fail loudly in `make worktree` / `make install` when the resolved interpreter is\n   outside the CI matrix, naming the mismatch. Cheapest, and mirrors the git-floor pattern\n   980d already established.\n3. Add the host's version to the CI matrix -- rejected as stated, since it grows CI cost for\n   every contributor to accommodate one machine, and does not stop the next drift.\n\nOption 2 alone leaves the developer to act; option 1 removes the failure mode. They compose.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] A venv created by `make worktree` runs a Python version the CI matrix actually tests, or\n      the command fails/warns explicitly naming the mismatch and the supported set.\n- [x] The supported set is single-sourced from the CI matrix rather than duplicated, so adding\n      or dropping a version in CI cannot silently desynchronize the check (the same\n      single-sourcing discipline as `.github/module-size-limit.txt` and\n      `.github/git-version-floor.txt`).\n- [x] A test proves the check fires for an out-of-matrix interpreter and stays silent for an\n      in-matrix one.\n- [x] `docs/local-dev-env.md` states the supported Python versions and how to provision a\n      matching venv when the host default differs.\n- [x] The two failures listed above are re-checked on an in-matrix interpreter and either\n      confirmed genuinely pre-existing or filed as real bugs -- right now nobody knows which,\n      because every observation of them has been on an untested Python.\n\n## Provenance\n\nSurfaced by the a573 agent, which noticed its worktree venv was 3.14.6 while explaining three\nlocal-only test failures. Independently verified here: system python3, the Makefile line, three\nworktree venvs, and the CI matrix all measured directly."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786098442056367001,"uuid":"1f1b60d5-45f5-40c2-a01e-8ae1de4322ac"}